12	description: |
13	  Implement batched square compact-Householder QR factorization.
14	
15	  Input is `A`, a `batch x n x n` CUDA tensor in `torch.float32`.
16	
17	  Return `(H, tau)` in the same compact Householder convention as
18	  `torch.geqrf(A)`. `H` is a `batch x n x n` FP32 tensor containing `R` in its
19	  upper triangle and Householder vectors below the diagonal. `tau` is a
20	  `batch x n` FP32 tensor containing reflector coefficients. The checker
21	  materializes `Q = torch.linalg.householder_product(H, tau)`, uses
22	  `R_factor = triu(H)`, and validates the LAPACK-style QR factorization
23	  residual `R_factor - Q.T @ A` and orthogonality of `Q`. Since `R_factor`
24	  is extracted with `triu`, triangularity is part of the factorization check:
25	  if `Q.T @ A` has meaningful lower-triangular leakage, then it cannot match
26	  `R_factor`. The checker reports that lower-triangular leakage and the
27	  reconstruction residual as diagnostics.
28	
29	  This shape set targets optimizer-style matrix statistics where gradients are
30	  viewed as `[for_each..., basis_dim, contracted_dim]`, statistics are formed as
31	  `G @ G.T`, and QR is run on square `basis_dim x basis_dim` matrices. Batched
32	  `512 x 512` is especially important, while `1024`, `2048`, and `4096` cover
33	  larger square factors.
34	
35	  Test and benchmark specs include a `cond` field. In this task `cond` is a
36	  deterministic input-scaling knob, not an exact requested condition number:
37	  dense cases multiply columns by `logspace(0, -cond, n)`, so larger `cond`
38	  creates a wider dynamic range across columns. Some stress cases use their own
39	  structure, such as rank-deficient, near-rank-deficient, banded, row-scaled,
40	  near-collinear, upper-triangular, or clustered-scale inputs.
41	
42	  The `mixed` case builds a heterogeneous batch: each matrix is independently
43	  assigned a conditioning profile (a well-conditioned dense majority interleaved
44	  with the ill-conditioned stress structures above) at a random position in the
45	  batch. This mirrors the real optimizer-statistics regime, where the per-layer
46	  or per-block factors batched into one call have widely varying conditioning,
47	  rather than all sharing one structure. The benchmark set (not just the test
48	  set) now includes both `mixed` batches and fully ill-conditioned homogeneous
49	  batches, so conditioning robustness is ranked, not only gated: an
50	  implementation cannot inspect a few matrices, decide the whole batch is
51	  well-conditioned, and route it to a path that is only valid for well-conditioned
52	  inputs, and the runtime cost of the accurate path on hard inputs is part of the
53	  score. Each matrix must be factored correctly on its own merits.
54	
55	  Correctness is a hard gate against the original FP32 input and the FP32
56	  `torch.geqrf` compact-factor contract. Low-bit FP16, FP8, or NVFP4 work is
57	  allowed only as an internal implementation strategy: returned factors must
58	  still be FP32 and must satisfy the same QR invariants as an FP32
59	  factorization. Residuals are measured in FP64 to reduce checker noise, but
60	  the target tolerance is still FP32 accuracy. The numerical property tolerance
61	  is purely relative, with no QR `atol`.
62	  The hard gates are the LAPACK-style factor residual, which uses
63	  `rtol = 20 * n * eps32`, and orthogonality, which uses
64	  `rtol = 100 * n * eps32`, each applied to the corresponding matrix L1 norm.
65	  Triangularity is reported as lower-triangular leakage in `Q.T @ A` and is
66	  already implied by the factor residual against `triu(H)`.
67	
68	  Among passing submissions, ranking is by runtime using the geometric mean of
69	  benchmark cases. We will also celebrate notable submissions beyond the main
70	  leaderboard: the fastest, the most elegant, and the strangest working kernels.
